1. Write a simple Java program where a client connects to a server and sends text messages.
The server must read each message and send it straight back until the client types "bye" to close the connection.

Execution Steps
Create two Java files named Server.java and Client.java.
Copy the server code into Server.java.
Copy the client code into Client.java.

Compile both programs:

javac Server.java
javac Client.java

Open the first terminal and run the server:

java Server

Open another terminal and run the client:

java Client
Type a message in the client window.
The server receives the message and sends the same message back.
Continue sending messages until "bye" is entered.
Both client and server terminate the connection.

Server.java
import java.io.*;
import java.net.*;


class Server {
    public static void main(String args[]) throws Exception {

        ServerSocket ss = new ServerSocket(5000);
        Socket s = ss.accept();

        BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(s.getInputStream()));
        PrintWriter pw = new PrintWriter(s.getOutputStream(), true);

        String msg;

        while (!(msg = br.readLine()).equals("bye")) {
            pw.println(msg);
        }

        pw.println("bye");

        s.close();
        ss.close();
    }
}

Client.java
import java.io.*;
import java.net.*;
import java.util.*;

class Client {
    public static void main(String args[]) throws Exception {

        Socket s = new Socket("localhost", 5000);

        BufferedReader br = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(s.getInputStream()));
        PrintWriter pw = new PrintWriter(s.getOutputStream(), true);

        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);

        String msg;

        do {
            System.out.print("Enter: ");
            msg = sc.nextLine();
            pw.println(msg);
            System.out.println("Server: " + br.readLine());
        } while (!msg.equals("bye"));

        s.close();
    }
}
